## PC & Browser Control

Three MCP servers grant browser and full-desktop control. Prefer the most specific tool for the job.

| Task | Use |
|---|---|
| Tabs, forms, scraping, general browsing | `playwright` (browser_* tools) |
| Slow pages, Core Web Vitals, memory leaks, CSS debugging | `chrome-devtools` (trace, lighthouse, heapsnapshot) |
| Clicking native Windows controls by name | `uia-mcp` (uia_find_name, uia_action) |
| Anything non-accessible (canvas, games, remote desktop) | `computer-use` (screenshot, click, type) |

`playwright` uses a persistent profile — logins and cookies survive between sessions.

## Parallelism

When a request needs **more than two tasks**, branch the work instead of running it serially. Start long, blocking work (model/dependency installs, renders, downloads, transcription) in the background and immediately continue with independent work — never sit idle waiting on a long install when the next step does not depend on it. Prefer `task` with `subagent_type: "general"` for a genuinely independent branch, and batch independent tool calls in a single message.

These can click and type into any window, including logged-in accounts, with no confirmation step. Confirm with the user before acting on anything irreversible (purchases, deletes, sending, posting). Prefer `playwright`/`uia-mcp` over raw pixel-clicking whenever possible — they target real elements and are far more reliable.
